Agent team bus: joining a team as a `vm` member
===============================================

A `vm` member is a Claude Code agent running inside a virtual machine (a libvirt guest,
for example). This file is the short version; the full guide, including what joining a
team grants and its limits, is docs/agent-bus.md in the fedora-desktop repository.

REQUIRED: gh in the guest, logged in to github.com as the agent user. pingbus checks every
reference at GitHub with gh's login and nothing else; without it `pingbus config check` and
`agent-bus-claude` refuse (exit 78): "gh is required and must be logged in: install gh and
run `gh auth login --hostname github.com`". Setting this up is yours; nothing here does it.

1. The kit inside the guest. Copy this kit, /usr/local/share/agent-bus/kit/, to the same
   path inside the guest and link its `pingbus` and `agent-bus-claude` into
   /usr/local/bin. The guest needs Python 3.11 or later. `pingbus` starts the interpreter
   its first line names (it does not look python3 up on PATH), which is /usr/bin/python3 as
   built; where that is older (EL9's is 3.9), name a newer one with
   `python3.N -m zipapp ./pingbus -p /usr/bin/python3.N -o pingbus.new` (3.11 or later) and
   put pingbus.new in its place. `pingbus` refuses an older one (exit 78).

2. The network. A guest on the homeserver's own machine reaches the bus address,
   `<bus_ip>`. On a libvirt bridge (qemu:///system's default network) it is routed through
   the bridge, so the team file's `allow_from` must list that network's subnet (not yet
   exercised by any acceptance run). On user-mode networking (passt, as rootless
   qemu:///session guests and the vmtest lab use) its connections leave from the host
   itself, as a ccy session's do, so that the homeserver sees `<bus_ip>`, which it always
   allows, and nothing is added (the member's first `pingbus recv` joining the team
   confirms it for your guest). A guest
   elsewhere reaches the team's WireGuard address, `<wg_ip>`, and `allow_from` lists the
   WireGuard subnet or the guest's peer address.

3. The member's arguments. Inside the guest, as the agent user, in its checkout:

   ```
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role> pingbus suggest-handle
   ```

   prints `--repo=... --host=... --type=vm`.

4. The bundle. On the team's homeserver host, a human with sudo runs:

   ```
   sudo agent-bus add-member <team> --repo=<repo> --host=<role> --type=vm --role=worker --address=<bus_ip> --out=$HOME/bundle-<team>
   ```

   (`--address=<wg_ip>` for a guest that comes over WireGuard; `--no-human-text` for pings
   only). Copy the bundle into the guest as ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/<team>/, owned
   by the agent user, directory 0700 and files 0600, and delete the copy left behind.

5. The env file, ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/env inside the guest:

   ```
   PINGBUS_TEAMS=<team>
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role>
   ```

6. Check and start. As the agent user, `pingbus config check` must print no error; then
   start sessions with `agent-bus-claude`. The agent user must not hold a human's Matrix
   session, here as anywhere.
